{0 The platform and the renderers}

A machine is a value. A host runs it: it keeps the snapshot, sends events,
paints the view and carries out the commands. Silica has three hosts that run
components: the browser, the server render and the memory platform of the
tests. The checker is a fourth host, which runs one machine at a time (see
{!page-checker}).

{1:instances The instance tree}

Every host that renders components uses one instance tree,
{!Silica.Instance}. It starts the machine of each component node that a view
holds, gives the props, takes the output to the parent, and ends a child that
is no longer rendered. [Instance.update] delivers one message and gives the
resolved view (with no component nodes left) and the commands of the step:

{@ocaml[
# let tree = Silica.Instance.mount Doc_party.component;;
val tree :
  (Doc_party.Party_machine.snapshot, Doc_party.event) Silica.Instance.t =
  <abstr>
# Silica.Render.to_string (fst (Silica.Instance.update tree None));;
- : string =
"<div><div><span>Adults: 0</span><button aria-label=\"Fewer Adults\" disabled>-</button><button aria-label=\"More Adults\">+</button></div><div><span>Children: 0</span><button aria-label=\"Fewer Children\" disabled>-</button><button aria-label=\"More Children\">+</button></div><p>0 guests</p></div>"
# Silica.Instance.count tree;;
- : int = 3
]}

The tree has three instances: the party and its two steppers.

{1:runtime The runtime and the platform}

{!Silica.Runtime} is the host runtime: the queue of events, the instance
tree, timers, Remote calls, Live subscriptions, the error page and the replay
of the answers of a server render. It reaches the screen and the network only
through one value of {!Silica.Platform.t}:

- [renderer]: a {!Silica.Renderer.t}. It paints views, turns platform input
  into events, runs the platform commands (focus, scroll, history, title) and
  starts and stops the held sources. It reports each URL change and each link
  preload with [on_route].
- [location]: the URL of the page now.
- [io]: a clock ([schedule]), one HTTP request form for Remote calls ([post]),
  a Live socket ([connect]), the CSRF token of the page, a hook for a session
  that the server no longer knows, and a log.

A platform for another screen writes one value of this type and no runtime
code. The decoding of answers, the replay of server answers, the reconnect
and the heartbeat of Live sockets are the same for every platform.

{1:browser The browser}

{!Silica_browser} ([silica.browser], Melange only) is the browser platform:
the runtime with a renderer for the DOM.

- {!Silica_browser.mount} paints a component into the element with an id.
- {!Silica_browser.mount_app} does the same for a router app: link presses,
  back and forward reach the router (see {!page-router}).
- {!Silica_browser.mount_live} renders a Live component whose machine runs on
  the server (see {!page-hydration.islands}).
- {!Silica_browser.mount_with_renderer} runs the same runtime with another
  {!Silica.Renderer}.
- {!Silica_browser.cookie} reads a cookie before the mount, so that the first
  props equal those of the server render.

The first paint adopts the HTML of the server instead of building it again
(see {!page-hydration}). After it, the root element gets the attribute
[data-silica-ready]. Events are delegated: for a DOM event, the renderer finds
the handler of the nearest element in the view.

{1:server The server render}

{!Silica_server} renders a page with the same instance tree: it mounts the
root at the URL, runs the Remote calls of the first render against the
handlers, and prints the view with {!Silica.Render.to_string}. It is not a
{!Silica.Platform.t}: it keeps no page after the answer. Most commands do
nothing there; [Set_title], [Set_status], [Set_document_attribute] and the
navigation commands change the answer. See {!page-server.render}.

{1:memory The memory platform}

{!Silica_memory} ([silica.memory], native only) is a platform with no DOM. It
keeps the last painted view, a clock that moves only when the test says so,
the requests that the test answers, Live sockets that the test opens and
feeds, and a history of URLs. It runs the same runtime as the browser, so a
test on native OCaml goes through the same queue, timers, calls and error
page. See {!page-testing.memory}.
